.TH ACCESS 2
.SH NAME
access \- determine accessibility of file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B "access(name, mode)"
.B "char *name;"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B access
checks the given file
.I name
for accessibility according to
.IR mode ,
which is 4 (read), 2 (write) or 1 (execute) or a combination thereof.
Specifying mode 0 tests whether the directories leading to the file can be
searched and the file exists.
.PP
An appropriate error indication is returned if
.I name
cannot be found or if
any of the desired access modes would not be granted.
On disallowed
accesses \-1 is returned and the error code is in
.BR errno .
0 is returned
from successful tests.
.PP
The user and group IDs with respect to which permission is checked are
the real UID and GID of the process, so this call is useful to set-UID
programs.
.PP
Notice that it is only access bits that are checked.
A directory may be
announced as writable by
.BR access ,
but an attempt to open it for writing
will fail (although files may be created there); a file may look executable,
but
.B exec
will fail unless it is in proper format.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
access(1), stat(2)
.SH ASSEMBLER
(access = 33.)
.TP
PDP-11:
.nf
sys access; name; mode
.fi
.TP
8086:
.nf
BX = 33
AX = name
DX = mode
int 0Xf1
.fi
.sp
